10. AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall)

AWS WAF helps protect web applications and APIs against common web exploits and unwanted web traffic.

It can be associated with supported resources such as Amazon CloudFront, Application Load Balancers, and Amazon API Gateway.

Key Features

* Filter HTTP and HTTPS requests.
* Create rules based on IP addresses, request characteristics, and other supported criteria.
* Use managed rule groups.
* Apply rate-based rules to help mitigate excessive request rates.
* Monitor matched requests through supported logging and metrics integrations.

Example

Suppose a website receives repeated suspicious requests targeting common web vulnerabilities. AWS WAF rules can block or count matching requests before they reach the application.

DevOps Use Case

Attach AWS WAF to a supported public-facing application entry point to help reduce malicious web traffic.

Best Practices

* Start with appropriate managed rule groups.
* Test rules in count mode before blocking when practical.
* Monitor false positives.
* Use rate-based rules where appropriate.
* Keep application-level authorization and validation in place.

Remember: WAF filters web requests; it does not replace secure application code.

