13. AWS Config

AWS Config continuously records supported resource configurations and evaluates them against desired configuration rules.

It helps determine whether AWS resources comply with organizational policies.

Key Features

* Track configuration changes.
* View configuration history.
* Evaluate resources against Config rules.
* Use managed or custom rules.
* Support compliance reporting.
* Integrate with remediation workflows.

Example

An AWS Config rule can detect an S3 bucket that does not meet a required encryption configuration.

DevOps Use Case

Use AWS Config to identify configuration drift and enforce cloud security standards across development, testing, and production accounts.

Example Rules

* S3 buckets should meet required encryption settings.
* EBS volumes should be encrypted.
* Required resource tags should be present.
* Security groups should not expose prohibited ports.

Actual rule availability and behavior depend on the selected AWS Config rule and resource type.

